<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canberra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canberra is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, built in the Australian Capital Territory as a compromise between the rival cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. The site was chosen in 1908 and named in 1913, and the city was planned by the American architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-burley-griffin">Walter Burley Griffin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marion-mahony-griffin">Marion Mahony Griffin</a> around an artificial lake. Parliament moved there from Melbourne in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canberra.jpg" alt="Canberra" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The 3B's · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_panorama_from_Mount_Ainslie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Canberra lies near the Brindabella Ranges, part of the Australian Alps, about 150 km inland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s east coast. Its urban area has an elevation of about 580 m, and the highest point near it is Mount Majura at 888 m. The Molonglo River was dammed to form Lake Burley Griffin, and it then flows into the Murrumbidgee. The area was a flood plain before the lake was filled.</p><p>The name derives from the Ngunnawal language of a local clan that the first British colonists called the Canberry, Kanberri or Nganbra tribe. Joshua John Moore, the first European landowner in the region, named his grant Canberry in 1823. The form Canberra began to appear around 1857. Suggested meanings include meeting place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first peoples of the area include the Ngunnawal and Ngambri. Archaeological evidence suggests human activity there 21,000 years ago. The Canberry or Nganbra clan lived mostly around Sullivan's Creek and had ceremonial grounds at the base of Galambary (Black Mountain), while the Pialligo clan had land around what is now Canberra Airport. The people managed the land with fire, farmed yams and hunted. Galambary was a meeting and business site, mainly for men, and an initiation site.</p><p>European settlement began in the first half of the 19th century. The federation of the Australian colonies was achieved on 1 January 1901, and the new capital was founded and formally named Canberra in 1913, as a compromise between Sydney and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. It is an entirely planned city, based on a design by the American architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-burley-griffin">Walter Burley Griffin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marion-mahony-griffin">Marion Mahony Griffin</a>, influenced by the garden city movement and aligned with landmarks such as Black Mountain, Mount Ainslie and Capital Hill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Canberra is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the largest population centre in the Australian Capital Territory, with an estimated 484,630 people. It is the country's largest inland city. It is the seat of the federal government, with Parliament House, Government House and the High Court, and it hosts all foreign embassies in Australia.</p><p>Among its national institutions are the Australian War Memorial, the Australian National University, established in 1946, the National Gallery, the National Museum and the National Library. The Royal Military College Duntroon is also there, and Floriade is the largest flower festival in the Southern Hemisphere.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canberra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The plan for <b>Canberra</b> came from an international competition won in 1912 by the American architect Walter Burley Griffin, with drawings by his wife and partner Marion Mahony Griffin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…3,400, about a third of them black volunteers, organised as light infantry for counter-insurgency; the Royal Rhodesian Air Force had 1,000 personnel, Hawker Hunter and de Havilland Vampire strike aircraft and <b>Canberra</b> bombers; the British South Africa Police fielded paramilitary units and the Central Intelligence Organisation gathered intelligence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…rights movement; the Aboriginal Land Rights Act followed in 1976, the Aboriginal Tent Embassy was set up in <b>Canberra</b> in 1972 to demand sovereignty, and in 1992 the Mabo Case declared terra nullius invalid and recognised native title, which the Native Title Act of 1993 put into statute.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2000 the volume holding Mark suffered what has been called minor pigment damage on its way to <b>Canberra</b>, perhaps from the vibration of the aeroplane's engines.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-kells">Book of Kells · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
